<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Cochin</h1><p class="st-meta">1102 CE – 1949 CE (approx.) · capital Thrippunithura</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Kerala kingdom of Kochi, whose early alliance with the Portuguese made its harbour the first European foothold in India and whose rulers reigned until 1949.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The ruling house, the Perumpadappu Swaroopam, emerged as one of the successor chiefdoms after the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chera-perumals-of-makotai">Chera Perumals of Makotai</a> collapsed in the early 12th century. In the 14th century, after a flood of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/periyar-river">Periyar</a> opened a new harbour, the dynasty made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Kochi</a> its centre, and the port grew as older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muziris">Muziris</a> declined.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin's</a> rulers were long overshadowed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorins</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-calicut">Kingdom of Calicut</a>, so in 1500 they welcomed the Portuguese, who built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-manuel">Fort Manuel</a> at Kochi in 1503. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> replaced them in 1663. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">Pepper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> drove the economy, and Kochi was home to Syrian Christians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Konkani merchants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakthan-thampuran">Sakthan Thampuran</a> (r. 1790–1805) curbed the nobles and rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrissur">Thrissur</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After invasions by Mysore, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a> became a British subsidiary ally from 1791 and was known for its schools and administration. In 1949 it merged with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/travancore">Travancore</a> to form <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/travancore-cochin">Travancore-Cochin</a>, which became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> in 1956.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-cochin.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Cochin" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cochin_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1102 CE – 1949 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Thrippunithura</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sakthan Thampuran, Rama Varma XV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malayalam</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cochin State, Perumpadappu Swaroopam, Kochi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1526&amp;focus=kingdom-of-cochin" aria-label="Kingdom of Cochin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6785 1806 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Cochin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7105 2062L7107 1971M7105 2062L7364 1601M7105 2062L7364 1602M7105 2062L7126 2117"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7107 1971h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7126 2117h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7105 2062h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7105 2062h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1526&amp;focus=kingdom-of-cochin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
